    <h2>Got Questions?</h2>


    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>I already sleep eight hours and I still wake up tired.</summary>
      <p>Then the hours were never the problem. Sleep isn't a timer - it's work your brain has to finish overnight, and three systems decide whether that work gets done. Out of sync, you get enough hours but zero rest: still foggy by noon, still dragging through your to-do list, and another week of it is another week of half-finished tasks. A tracker will say the night was bad and stop there. The protocol says which of the three systems is broken, in what order to fix it, and what to change tonight - and if thirty days don't make your hours sharper, the guarantee above covers it.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>How much time does this actually take?</summary>
      <p>Most videos run ten to fifteen minutes, a few go longer. Then a two-minute diary entry in the morning, and an action card that tells you exactly what to do. No 5 AM club, no two-hour wind-down routines.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>I've tried everything. Why would this be different?</summary>
      <p>If your list looks like standing desks, pomodoro, power naps, more caffeine, even a vacation you came back from feeling worse - that list manages your daytime. The protocol rebuilds the thing every one of those tools depends on: how your brain recovers at night. And if I'm wrong about you, the guarantee above means you don't pay for my mistake.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Isn't the 2 PM crash just my lunch?</summary>
      <p>You're close, and lighter lunches do take the edge off. But the dip everybody gets in the early afternoon lives in your clock, not in your lunch - a heavy meal deepens it, it doesn't create it. And what you reach for at midday was already decided the night before. You didn't eat wrong. You slept short, and then ate the way a short-slept body makes you eat. Eating lighter buys back part of one afternoon - and the second half of your day is the busy half. Four weeks of the protocol goes after the night that sets it, so the hours are there when you need them.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>My schedule isn't mine - kids, meetings, deadlines.</summary>
      <p>One fixed point, and that's all the protocol asks - and it isn't your bedtime, we never hand you one. Everything else runs on windows you place around your day, not a schedule we hand you. By the final week those windows are written into your personal protocol card, built around your days, not someone else's.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Do I have to give up coffee?</summary>
      <p>No. I drink it, I like it, and nothing here ends with you quitting. But the cup that used to switch your brain on barely moves it now, and that isn't because you got more tired - there's a reason, and it's fixable. The protocol hands that cup back. Nothing to buy, nothing to add, no earlier alarm - and Your Caffeine Blueprint sets it to what you actually drink.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>How exactly does the refund work?</summary>
      <p>All thirty days are unconditional - no questions, nothing to send, not even the diary. Just tell me it didn't work and you get every cent back. If you did keep the diary and want to go through it with me, I'll look at your wake times - never at what you wrote in the notes - but that's help, not a requirement. After day 30 it closes. Complete terms are in the <a href="/refunds/">refund policy</a>.</p>
    </details>
